          "text": "Let me recall the actual situation. The user says they think the fix was about `${args}` vs `\"${args}\"`, previously quoted, changed to unquoted.\n\nLet me look at the journal entries again. From 2026-09-10:\n\"причина: на виндоус-ветке run_case аргументы парсились по пробелу, а в линукс-ветк�� - отдавались массивом. поэтому \"\" парсился как нуль, \"2 3\" - как не-числовой аргумент.\nфикс: в tests/CMakeLists.txt убрала кавычки от args в add_test - так оно развернётся в отдельные аргументы. tests/cases.cmake - убрала кавычки от set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\") - также чтобы 2 и 3 были отдельными аргументами\"\n\nAnd 2026-09-14 goes deeper into the arg parsing difference and ends up making run_case quote each argument in the Windows branch.\n\nSo the situation: Initially the test `RAD_TOO_MUCH` was `\"2 3\"` (quoted) and in `add_test` args was passed as `\"${args}\"`. The issue was that on Linux, args are passed as an array (execv), so `\"2 3\"` becomes a single argument with a space, rather than two separate args. On Windows, args are concatenated into a command line string, so the space acted as a separator.\n\nThe fix: remove quotes so that `2` and `3` are separate CMake list items → separate arguments on both platforms.\n\nWait, but there's nuance. `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\")` sets a single string \"2 3\". Then `${RAD_TOO_MUCH}` in CMake unquoted expands to... a single argument \"2 3\" (spaces in CMake don't split expansion of variables after the fact - actually CMake variable expansion does NOT split on spaces; semicolons are list separators). So `${RAD_TOO_MUCH}` = \"2 3\" as one argument. `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)` sets a list with two elements \"2;3\". Then `${RAD_TOO_MUCH}` unquoted expands to two arguments \"2\" and \"3\".\n\nAnd `\"${args}\"` quoted keeps it as one argument; `${args}` unquoted... hmm, in CMake, unquoted `${args}` where args is \"2;3\" expands to two arguments. But wait, the key CMake subtlety: unquoted variable expansion splits on semicolons (list separators), NOT spaces. So `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\")` + `${RAD_TOO_MUCH}` gives one argument \"2 3\". To get two args you need `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)`.\n\nNow regarding `args` in add_case: it's a function parameter. If you pass `${RAD_TOO_MUCH}` unquoted at the call site, then inside the function `args` will be one argument \"2 3\" (single string). Then in add_test, `${args}` unquoted gives \"2 3\" as one arg (because no semicolon), `\"${args}\"` also \"2 3\" as one arg. Hmm.\n\nActually wait. Let me think more carefully. The CMake function `add_case(test_name exe args input ...)` — positional arguments. If you call `add_case(t4_c_radius_too_much t4_c \"${RAD_TOO_MUCH}\" ...)`, and RAD_TOO_MUCH is \"2 3\", then args = \"2 3\" (single argument with space). If you call `add_case(... ${RAD_TOO_MUCH} ...)` unquoted where RAD_TOO_MUCH = \"2;3\", then... positional args: the function would receive more than the expected number of args! Because \"2\" and \"3\" would become two separate arguments, shifting everything.\n\nHmm, actually that's an important subtlety. Let me reconsider.\n\nFor functions with fixed positional params, passing an unquoted list of 2 elements where 1 param is expected shifts the subsequent params.\n\nLet me re-read the cases.cmake: line 192 `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)` — this is a list \"2;3\". Line 226: `add_case(t4_c_radius_too_much t4_c \"${RAD_TOO_MUCH}\" t4_bad_radius ...)`. Here `\"${RAD_TOO_MUCH}\"` — quoted! So it's passed as a single argument \"2 3\"? Wait, quoted expansion of a list joins with semicolons: `\"${RAD_TOO_MUCH}\"` where RAD_TOO_MUCH=\"2;3\" yields the single string \"2;3\". Hmm, so args = \"2;3\".\n\nThen in add_test line 72: `${args}` unquoted → expands list \"2;3\" → two arguments \"2\" \"3\". That's the fix! Whereas `\"${args}\"` would give one argument \"2;3\".\n\nSo the user's memory: previously `\"${args}\"` in add_test (quoted), changed to `${args}` unquoted. Yes it appears line 73 comment confirms: \"args без кавычек чтобы не ломалось на пустоте и на 'лишних' аргументах\".\n\nBut wait, the set in cases.cmake is now `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)` (unquoted, a list), and passed as `\"${RAD_TOO_MUCH}\"` (quoted, but a list joined with `;`). So the combination quoted-at-callsite + unquoted-at-add_test works because CMake lists use `;` as separator.\n\nHmm, so the user's statement \"раньше было второе, поменяла на первое\" (previously the quoted version, changed to unquoted) — correct about add_test's `${args}`.\n\nBut there's a subtlety they may be conflating: `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\")` → `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)`. The journal on 09-10 said \"убрала кавычки от set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\")\". On 09-14, they changed it back to test hypothesis? Actually on 09-14:\n\"для проверки изменю вот так: set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3) -> set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\"). тесты под виндой не должны изменить поведения. а по линуксом будет падение\"\n\nHmm wait, that suggests at that point the file had `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)` and they temporarily changed to quoted to test. So the final state is `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)`.\n\nBut then 09-14 says: \"защищать аргументы в виндоус-ветке экранированными кавычками... так и есть, эти два теста упали... попросила deepseek предложить более идиоматичный вариант и исправила на него склейку argv для ребёнка\". So the final fix was in run_case.cpp (Windows branch) — quoting each argv element when building the command line, rather than the CMake quoting. That's an important part the user may be forgetting.\n\nLet me look at run_case.cpp to confirm the current state.\n\nActually let me focus on answering accurately. The user's question: \"напомни ситуацию, когда в tests/CMakeLists.txt у меня была ситуация разъезда аргумента с пробелом. я думаю это про ${args} или \"${args}\", раньше было второе, поменяла на первое. т.к. иначе два арг. склеиваются в один и тогда будет проблема. верно помню?\"\n\nSo they want me to verify. The core: yes, in add_test `${args}` is now unquoted (line 72), and there's a comment on line 73 justifying it. Previously it was `\"${args}\"`.\n\nBut I should correct a nuance: the actual \"разъезд\" (divergence) was a platform difference: on Linux args go as an array (execv), on Windows they're a concatenated command line string. The root cause was HOW arguments reach the child. The `\"${args}\"` + `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\")` made \"2 3\" one argument; on Linux that's one arg with a space, on Windows the space became a separator.\n\nLet me check run_case.cpp to see the Windows arg quoting fix.\n\nActually let me read run_case.cpp.\n\nAlso check the journal: on 09-10 the reason given was: \"на виндоус-ветке run_case аргументы парсились по пробелу, а в линукс-ветке - отдавались массивом. поэтому \"\" парсился как нуль, \"2 3\" - как не-числовой аргумент.\"\n\nAnd on 09-14, the deeper analysis and final fix.\n\nLet me read run_case.cpp.",
