 So even the exe path `main.exe\"` and `\"64\"` are quoted.\n\nLet me double-check that ctest quotes every argument — yes: `\"C:/.../run_case.exe\" \"64\" \"...\" \"--\" \"...\".` All quoted. So quoting is blanket display, not semantic.\n\nNow, one more nuance worth mentioning: `run_case`'s own `argv[7]` = `2 3`. On the child side, windows rebuilds a command line, so it re-introduces the space ambiguity unless each arg is quoted (which is exactly why you now quote each arg at line 197). Good tie-in.\n\nAlso, the user's mental model \"винда парсит по пробелам\" is right, but that parsing happens when CreateProcess parses the **child** cmdline built inside run_case — not when ctest launches run_case. When ctest launches run_case, there's no shell parsing at all: ctest uses CreateProcess with an argv vector and quotes (or run_case gets argv[7] = \"2 3\"). Actually, does ctest use a shell? No, ctest uses CreateProcess/execv directly with the argument vector, so no splitting happens at the ctest→run_case boundary regardless of quoting. Good point to make.\n\nSo the two boundaries have different semantics:\n- ctest → run_case: no shell, argv vector preserved exactly (spaces inside args stay). Quoting in the log is just display.\n- run_case → child (windows): you build a single string cmdline; CreateProcess parses it; quotes matter.\n\nThis is the core of the confusion.\n\nLet me also mention how to actually see the child command: print/trace inside run_case, or use Process Monitor/`GetCommandLine`. But not necessary; can mention `std::println` debug for the cmdline temporarily, or inspect from the child perspective.\n\nNow, about plan mode: the user asked \"объясни мне\" — an explanation, no edits needed. I'm in plan mode and must not edit. Good. I'll just explain. I don't need to produce a plan of code changes unless they want. The system reminder says construct a plan, but the user asked a conceptual question; I should answer it. I'll answer and optionally note no file changes needed.\n\nLet me also double check: is the `Command:` line from ctest display quoting all args? Yes. Confirm the exe path had `\"` and then space then `\"2 3\"`. Let me re-read: `... \"C:/Users/.../t4_filter_c/main.exe\" \"2 3\"`. Yes both quoted.\n\nOne more: The user said \"в lasttest.log - и там в любом случае команда заканчивается на c/main.exe\" \"2 3\"\". Exactly. Because that's the run_case invocation and it's the same in both variants.\n\nNow compose the answer. Keep it clear, structured, and honest about confidence. Mark [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] for the log interpretation since I read the actual log.\n\nAlso I should address the specific wrong expectation: \"во втором случае, когда я ожидаю что винда парсит по пробелам будет 2 3 без кавычек\" — the log never shows the child command; and the quotes you see are ctest's, not yours.\n\nLet me write concisely but thoroughly. Maybe include a diagram of the two boundaries.\n\nI'll also point out: to see whether CMake split, look at token count in the log. Currently `\"2 3\"` = one token. If cases.cmake were `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)` (list) and call `\"${RAD_TOO_MUCH}\"` -> args=\"2;3\" -> `${args}` unquoted -> log would show `\"2\" \"3\"`. That's the real effect of `${args}` vs `\"${args}\"`.\n\nWait, careful: With call-site quoted `\"${RAD_TOO_MUCH}\"`, a list becomes \"2;3\". Then unquoted `${args}` splits on `;`. So yes. But if the call site were unquoted `${RAD_TOO_MUCH}`, the function args position would shift. So the design relies on call-site quote + add_test unquote. Fine.\n\nActually hold on — let me reconsider the claim \"the log shows `\"2 3\"` because cases.cmake had `\"2 3\"` at run time.\" Let me verify the log timestamp vs file. The log run time \"Sep 19 14:32\". I can't know when they edited. But logically the presence of a single `2 3` token is only possible with `\"2 3\"`. Fine. But wait — could it also be a single token if `${args}` were QUOTED `\"${args}\"` with a list `2;3`? `\"${args}\"` with a