так сказать, от того, что к ним придёт. если это 2 3 - они скажут \"лишнее\", но \"2 3\" - \"плохо т к строка\". при этом если    10 или \"   10\" - итог одинаков. мне кажется это нечестным.\"\n\nSo they're comparing:\n- `2 3` (two args) → \"лишнее\"\n- `\"2 3\"` (one arg) → \"строка\"\n- `   10` vs `\"   10\"` → same result\n\nThey think it's нечестно that the behavior depends on quoting for the middle-space case but not for the edge-space case.\n\nHmm, but actually: in the CMake test harness, can you even express `2 3` as two args? Yes, via list `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)`. And `\"2 3\"` as one arg via `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\")`.\n\nBut the user is conflating two levels:\n1. The CMake test harness decides how many argv elements the program gets.\n2. The program's parser handles each element.\n\nThe \"честность\" is about the **semantics of argv**: a CLI deliberately receives an argv vector; whether a space is a separator was already decided by the *caller* (shell). The program is not responsible for that. So:\n- `   10`: the caller passed one token with leading spaces. The program trims → accepts. This is a deliberate design choice (leading/trailing whitespace tolerance).\n- `\"2 3\"`: the caller passed one token with inner spaces. The program can't parse it as a number. Deliberate: inner whitespace is not part of a valid numeric literal.\n- `2 3`: the caller passed two tokens. The program sees an unexpected extra positional argument. Deliberate: too many args.\n\nAll three are consistent under a single rule: the program treats each argv element independently, trims surrounding whitespace, and rejects non-numeric content. There's no contradiction. The apparent \"unfairness\" is just that `   10` and `10` are the *same token* modulo ignorable edge whitespace, while `\"2 3\"` and `2 3` are *different* inputs (one vs two elements) — so of course the outcomes differ.\n\nSo my response: it's not unfair, it's the standard argv contract. The program correctly treats the invocation shape as part of the input. Mark category.\n\nBut I should be critical and consider whether the user has a legitimate point. Is there any sense in which it IS unfair?\n\nPotential legitimate point: The tests are the ones creating the ambiguity. If `RAD_TOO_MUCH` is supposed to test \"too many arguments\", it should be a genuine list (`set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)`), not a single string with a space (`\"2 3\"`). Using `\"2 3\"` tests \"one malformed numeric token\", not \"too many args\". So there's a **test-design** confusion: the name `RAD_TOO_MUCH` and the expectation `t4_radius_too_much` (\"Ожидался радиус; были введены лишние аргументы\") only match when the value is a list of 2. The user's intentional experiment with `\"2 3\"` renamed the semantics of that test without renaming it. That's the real issue — not the program.\n\nAlso, the user says \"t4 зависят от того, что к ним придёт\" — yes, all CLI programs do. That's the point of argv.\n\nAnother angle: could one argue the parser should treat `2 3` inside one arg as \"too many numbers / extra data\"? No — because the program receives it as ONE argument (the radius slot). It has no second slot. It parses it as a number, fails → \"не число\". There's no basis to call it \"лишнее\", since \"лишнее\" is about argv count, not content. Actually, the message \"были введены лишние аргументы\" is specifically about argc. Content-level \"лишнее\" is a different error. So both are semantically distinct and correct.\n\nI think the user may be mixing \"unfair\" with \"surprising\". Let me address:\n- It's principled, not unfair. Explain the single rule.\n- The asymmetry is expected: edge whitespace is normalized away by design; the number of argv elements is a real, observable difference.\n- Where there might be a real problem: test design — `\"2 3\"` no longer tests \"too many args\"; it tests \"malformed single arg\". If they keep `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\")`, the test name/expectation is misleading.\n- Also, the user themselves deliberately chose `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\")`; the original correct version was `2 3` (list). So the \"нечестность\" they observe is manufactured by that intentional change.\n\nLet me also consider the user's phrase \"при этом если    10 или \"   10\" - итог одинаков\". Actually is that true at the argv level? `   10` as a single arg with leading spaces. If the test harness did `set(RAD_SPACE \"   10\")`, that's one token with leading spaces. There's no way to express `   10` as \"10 with spaces\" vs \"\\\"   10\\\"\" as different invocations at the argv level — both are one token with leading spaces. The quotes are just shell syntax to protect the spaces; the program never sees them. So indeed both give the same argv element `   10`, hence same result. That's not \"unfair\", it's that quoting is not part of the data — quotes are consumed by the caller.\n\nAh, this is the key insight the user might be missing: **quotes are not passed to the program.** They're syntax of the caller. So `\"2 3\"` and `2 3` are not \"the same string with/without quotes\" — they're **two different argument vectors**: `[\"2 3\"]` vs `[\"2\", \"3\"]`. Whereas `\"   10\"` and `   10` are the **same argument vector**: `[\"   10\"]` — the quotes only protect the spaces, they don't change the element.\n\nWait, is `   10` (unquoted) even legal as one arg? In a shell, `   10` unquoted would be split into... no. In the CMake test we construct argv directly, so quoting is a CMake thing. But conceptually: quotes group; removing them lets the shell split. In CMake, `\"2 3\"` is one element, `2 3` (as two list-ish tokens) is two. And `\"   10\"` vs `   10`: CMake both are one element; `   10` unquoted in CMake is still one element (CMake doesn't split on spaces!). Right — in CMake, spaces in a literal argument don't split after expansion. So `set(RAD_SPACE \"   10\")` and using it as `\"${RAD_SPACE}\"` gives one element `   10`. There's no \"unquoted\" variant that splits it, because CMake doesn't split on spaces.\n\nHold on, but the user wrote `   10` (without quotes) as if a case. In CMake, if you write `set(RAD_SPACE    10)` that's still one element `10`? Actually `set(RAD_SPACE    10)` — multiple spaces collapse; it's one argument `10`. Hmm. So `   10` isn't expressible as a CMake literal with leading spaces unless quoted. But it doesn't matter; the point is the argv element is the same.\n\nSo the \"asymmetry\" the user perceives is actually:\n- For `2 3`: there are two *different argv vectors* possible: `[\"2\",\"3\"]` (needs list) and `[\"2 3\"]` (needs quote). Different → different outcome. Fair.\n- For `   10`: there's only *one* argv vector `[\"   10\"]`; \"quoted vs not\" doesn't produce a different vector. Same → same outcome. Fair.\n\nBoth are consistent. It's \"нечестно\" only if you think quotes are part of the data.\n\nLet me now think about whether there's a genuine design question worth raising: Should t4 treat a single arg containing internal whitespace `\"2 3\"` as \"too many args\" instead of \"not a number\"? I'd say no:\n- \"too many args\" is defined at argc level; at the time it checks argc it sees exactly 2 args (exe + R), so no extra. Then parsing R fails.\n- Alternatively, one could split R by whitespace and re-count tokens. But that's over-engineering and introduces its own ambiguity (what about `1 2` as radius? clearly malformed). The standard CLI convention: one argv element = one argument. Don't re-parse.\n- Also `std::stod`/parser naturally stops at space / rejects.\n\nSo my verdict: the user's program behavior is correct and principled; the perceived unfairness stems from (a) treating quotes as data and (b) the test's name/value mismatch when they intentionally set `\"2 3\"`.\n\nLet me give a crisp answer with category/confidence.\n\nAlso I should note: \"нечестно\" — I can validate the underlying feeling that yes, the observable behavior depends on the *invocation*, and that's inherent to CLI. But it's not a defect.\n\nI could suggest: if they want a test for \"too many args\", use `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)` (list). If they want a test for \"single malformed numeric arg\", rename the case/expectation. Don't conflate. And they already have `t4_c_radius_too_much` expecting \"лишние\". So restoring list is the right move; the `\"2 3\"` experiment is a mislabel.\n\nWait — but earlier the user said \"намеренно сделала set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\")\" and observed \"строка\". And my file read showed `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)`. Confusing. Maybe they're describing an experiment and haven't committed/reverted. I'll not over-focus. I'll clarify the principle and the test-design consequence, and ask what they want (too much vs malformed) — since plan mode encourages clarifying.\n\nActually the user just asks \"что скажешь на мои рассуждения?\" — wants my opinion. Keep concise-ish, but they said \"кратко\" earlier for the previous question; this one didn't say кратко. But overall they like concise. Let me be moderately concise.\n\nStructure:\n1. Короткий вердикт: не нечестно, это контракт argv.\n2. Ключ: кавычки — это синтаксис вызывающего, в программу не попадают. `\"2 3\"` и `2 3` — это разные векторы (1 vs 2 элемента), а `\"   10\"` и `   10` — один и тот же вектор (1 элемент). Отсюда асимметрия — она честная.\n3. Единое правило программы: один argv-элемент = один аргумент; края триммятся; внутренние пробелы → не число. `2 3` → 2 элемента → лишнее. Всё согласовано.\n4. Где реальная проблема: тест. `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\")` перестаёт проверять \"лишние аргументы\" и начинает проверять \"кривое число\", но имя/expected остались от \"too much\". Это не дефект программы, а переименование смысла кейса. Если хочешь \"лишнее\" — список `2 3`; если хочешь \"строка\" — отдельный кейс с явным именем.\n5. Ask: какой из двух смыслов ты хочешь закрепить в `RAD_TOO_MUCH`?\n\nMark categories. [ФАКТ] argv contract. [МНЕНИЕ] that it's fair/correct design.\n\nI must not edit. Good.\n\nLet me write.\n\nOne more nuance: Both cases give exit 64 (usage). So at the машинный канал level they're identical; only the stderr differs. That's worth noting: the \"честность\" at code level is preserved; only human-readable detail differs. Actually is that right? \"Радиус должен быть числом\" → USAGE 64; \"лишние аргументы\" → USAGE 64. Yes both 64. So the machine channel is consistent, the human channel is correctly more specific. That actually supports \"it's fine\".\n\nWait, but is \"не число\" a usage error (64) or data (65)? For an **argument**, non-number is usage (64), because argv is usage. In t3/t4, radius arg non-number → 64 per задача 4 (\"Аргумент не задан / не число / не положителен → код 64\"). Yes. Good.\n\nSo both are 64 — machine channel identical. Good point to include.\n\nLet me finalize.",
