          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks a design question: if we remove the quoting in run_case (as they did), then on Windows `\"2 3\"` becomes `2 3` and stderr differs. They ask: is it normal that run_case carries such big responsibility for exactly what it passes? Or is it better not to rely on it and always check \"what's in the string\"?\n\nLet me think about this carefully. This is a design/architecture question about the test harness.\n\nKey point: run_case is an *adapter* between ctest's argv vector (Windows/Unix) and the child process. On Windows, since CreateProcess takes a single command-line string, ANY launcher must serialize the argv vector into a string. This is inherent to the platform, not specific to run_case. The question is whether that serialization is correct (lossless).\n\nThe correct serialization must be **injective**: distinct argv vectors must map to distinct command lines, and the child must reconstruct exactly the same argv vector. Windows has documented quoting rules (the CRT / CommandLineToArgvW rules). If you just concatenate without quoting, you lose the boundary: `[\"2 3\"]` and `[\"2\",\"3\"]` both produce `2 3` → the function is not injective → silent corruption. That's the bug the user found.\n\nSo run_case DOES have a real responsibility here, and it's unavoidable on Windows: it must round-trip argv. The fix (quoting each arg) is the correct standard approach. Removing the quotes is a regression, not a simplification. On Linux, execv takes the vector directly, so run_case just forwards `&argv[6]` — no responsibility, no possibility of corruption. The asymmetry is a platform property.\n\nNow the user's alternative: \"лучше не полагаться на него и в любом случае смотреть 'а что в строке'?\" — i.e., maybe the test cases should avoid relying on run_case's quoting and always look at the resulting string. Hmm. Let me interpret: they might mean: avoid depending on argv-shape-sensitive cases; or make checks based on the actual content.\n\nLet me think about what advice is right.\n\nOption A: Keep run_case's quoting (as currently at line 197). This makes run_case a faithful adapter. Then CMake test cases can express argv shape precisely: `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH 2 3)` = 2 args, `set(RAD_TOO_MUCH \"2 3\")` = 1 arg. Both platforms consistent. This is the principled solution.\n\nOption B: Remove quoting → run_case no longer round-trips → `\"2 3\"` silently becomes 2 args on Windows. This creates platform divergence again and hides the intent. Bad.\n\nSo the answer: no, don't remove the quoting. run_case's job IS to faithfully transport argv; that's exactly what a launcher/test-runner must do. It's not \"big responsibility\" that's unreasonable — it's the core correctness requirement of the adapter. And the way to make it trustworthy is not \"avoid relying on it\" but to make it correct and add a test for the round-trip.\n\nBut there's a subtlety worth surfacing: is `cmdline += \" \\\"\" + arg + \"\\\"\"` fully correct per Windows rules? Not necessarily — Windows quoting rules are more complex (backslashes before quotes, embedded quotes). For your test args (no embedded quotes, no trailing backslashes), simple quoting is fine. For full correctness you'd use a proper function (e.g., `CommandLineToArgvW` inverse / the \"quoted\" rules from `Parsing C++ command-line arguments`). But mark as [ФАКТ/МНЕНИЕ]. Since args are controlled test values, simple quoting is acceptable but worth documenting as a known limitation.\n\nAlso note: there's a deeper point. The user asks \"is it normal that such big responsibility is on run_case?\" Answer: yes, but only because Windows' process API forces a string. On POSIX it's zero responsibility. So the \"responsibility\" is a Windows platform tax, not an artifact of your design. Any test runner on Windows must solve it (GoogleTest, boost::process, Python subprocess all do this).\n\nNow, \"или лучше не полагаться на него и в любом случае смотреть 'а что в строке'?\" — I think they mean: maybe don't make test outcomes depend on quoting; instead always inspect the actual command string. But I'd push back: that would be a workaround that hides the problem. The right approach:\n1. Make run_case faithfully transport argv (quote each element).\n2. Add a **test for run_case itself** — a self-test that passes an arg with a space and asserts the child sees one element (and two elements for the list case). Then you don't have to \"trust\" run_case by faith; it's covered.\n3. Then test cases can rely on it.\n\nThat converts \"большая ответственность\" into \"проверенная ответственность\". This aligns with the course's § 4.9 (tests check all channels) and the philosophy of making the harness itself trustworthy.\n\nAlso the user's phrase \"в любом случае смотреть 'а что в строке'\" — maybe they mean: don't rely on the argv boundary; instead assert on stderr content only, which already reflects what the program saw. But that's circular: the stderr IS the observable, and it differs exactly because argv differs. So asserting on stderr is what you already do; the issue is whether the argv you intended actually arrives.\n\nLet me also consider: is there a case for NOT using argv with spaces at all, i.e., avoid `\"2 3\"` as a single arg in tests? If a test wants \"too many args\", use a genuine list (2 3 → two args). If it wants \"malformed number\", use a genuinely malformed token that isn't ambiguous with extra args, e.g., `4ch` (already `RAD_SYMB`) or `2,3` or `abc`. That avoids the whole class of ambiguity. Actually `RAD_TOO_MUCH`'s purpose is extra args; `RAD_SYMB` (`4ch`) already covers malformed. So `\"2 3\"` as one arg is a weird hybrid that tests nothing uniquely valuable — it tests \"number with internal space\". You could keep it as an explicit separate case if desired, but it's not the \"too much\" case.\n\nSo there are two layers:\n- Harness correctness: run_case must quote. Keep it. Don't remove.\n- Test-case hygiene: pick unambiguous inputs; name cases by real intent. Don't use `\"2 3\"` under the `_too_much` name.\n\nBoth matter. The user seems to be choosing between \"fix/handle the harness\" vs \"avoid relying on it\". My recommendation: do both — fix the harness (quote, and add a self-test), and keep test cases semantically clean so you're not depending on cross-platform quoting subtleties unnecessarily. Rely on the harness only after it's covered by a test.\n\nNow, plan mode: they might want a plan. The system reminder says construct a plan and ask clarifying questions. But the user's question is conceptual (\"это нормально? или лучше...?\"). I should answer conceptually and then maybe propose a plan direction, asking what they want. Since plan mode is active, I should not edit. I can present analysis + options and ask.\n\nLet me also verify current run_case state: it DOES quote (line 197). The user says \"давай скажем, что убрали экран. кавычки под виндой как я это делала\" — hypothetical: if we removed the quotes. So current code has quotes. Good.\n\nLet me think about whether quoting is sufficient for round-trip with the CRT parsing. Windows CreateProcess parses the command line using the rules of the program's startup (the child CRT, `CommandLineToArgvW`). The rules: whitespace separates tokens unless inside double quotes; backslash before quote has special meaning; `\"\"` inside quoted... For args without `\"` and not ending with `\\`, wrapping in `\"...\"` is correct. Test args here: `2 3`, `4ch`, `   10`, `0x10`, `-10`, `+10`, `++10`, `+-3`, `1e400`, etc. None contain `\"` or trailing backslash. So simple quoting is fine. But if a future test used an arg with a quote, it'd break. Mark as limitation.\n\nActually wait, there's a subtlety with `   10` (leading spaces) passed as a single arg: cmdline `\"   10\"` → quoted → child gets `   10` (spaces preserved). Good, that's why quoting matters for leading spaces too! Without quotes, `   10` would be... leading spaces stripped by parser → `10`. So actually the leading-space case DOES depend on quoting as well. Hmm, but the user said `   10` vs `\"   10\"` give the same result — that's because both are the same one element, and with quoting preserved. If quotes removed in run_case, `   10` → parser collapses leading spaces → still `10` → parser trims → same result. So the *program* result is same because the program trims. But the argv element would differ (`   10` vs `10`) — invisible because program normalizes. Interesting: so the leading-space test's *intent* (does the parser skip leading spaces?) is actually NOT testable via this harness if run_case preserves spaces... wait it is: with quoting, child receives `   10`; program trims → success. Without quoting, child receives `10` (spaces eaten by CreateProcess) → also success. So the test passes either way and doesn't actually prove the program trims leading spaces! That's the \"скрытая зависимость\" / test not testing what it claims. This is a great critical point.\n\nHmm, is that right? Let me double check Windows command line parsing. `2 3` unquoted splits by spaces. Leading spaces are just separators; the token is `10`. So `   10` unquoted → child argv `10`. With quotes `\"   10\"` → child argv `   10`. So the harness controls whether the program actually sees leading spaces. If run_case quotes (current), the child sees `   10` and the program's trim is genuinely exercised. If run_case didn't quote, the leading-space test would be vacuous. So quoting in run_case is actually necessary for the leading-space test to be meaningful too.\n\nThat's an important insight to share: the harness's quoting determines whether your parser-level tests are actually reachable. So \"not relying on run_case\" is impossible — the harness is part of the test's meaning. Therefore the harness must be correct and itself tested.\n\nBut wait — on Linux, execv passes `   10` as-is regardless of quoting, so Linux genuinely tests the trim. On Windows without quoting, it wouldn't. So the test would be green on Windows for the wrong reason. That's the kind of silent divergence the user has been fighting.\n\nSo my recommendation solidifies:\n1. Keep run_case quoting (faithful round-trip). It's not optional on Windows.\n2. Add a test for run_case itself: invoke a tiny probe that prints its own argc/argv, with cases: one arg with internal space, one arg with leading spaces, two args. Assert the child sees exactly the intended vector. This makes the adapter trustworthy by test, not by faith.\n3. Keep test cases semantically clean: `_too_much` = real extra args.\n\nAlternatively, if they don't want to build a probe, at minimum add a comment documenting that run_case guarantees argv round-trip, and why quoting is there.\n\nNow, is there any argument that run_case is doing \"too much\"? One could say: run_case's contract is \"given expected code, files, and a command with args, run it and capture\". Serializing argv is part of \"run it\". On Windows it's mandatory. So no, it's not overreach. The only criticism: maybe run_case should use a well-defined argv→cmdline function rather than ad-hoc formatting, to be correct for all inputs. Mark.\n\nLet me also address: \"или лучше не полагаться на него и в любом случае смотреть 'а что в строке'?\" — I read this as: maybe always inspect the actual received string rather than trusting the harness. My answer: the received string IS what you assert on (stderr), but that's downstream; the upstream must be guaranteed. You can't \"look at the string\" to detect a harness boundary bug unless you assert on argv shape, which requires a probe. So: trust by test.\n\nAlso relevant: the course philosophy § 4.9.7 \"Тесты проверяют все три канала\" and the idea that the harness itself must be honest (make test returns non-zero). run_case is the harness's core; it deserves its own coverage.\n\nNow, plan mode: I should present a plan or ask. The user asked a yes/no-ish design question. I'll give the analysis and propose a direction, then ask whether they want me to prepare a concrete plan (after plan mode). Since I'm to construct a well-formed plan, I can outline it and ask for confirmation. But the user may just be discussing. Let me give a strong opinion + options + a question.\n\nLet me keep it reasonably concise but substantive. Use categories.\n\nLet me draft:\n\nVerdict: не убирать кавычки. Это не «большая ответственность», а обязательный контракт адаптера на Windows.\n\nWhy: CreateProcess принимает строку, поэтому любой launcher на Windows обязан сериализовать argv. Без кавычек сериализация неинъективна: `[\"2 3\"]` и `[\"2\",\"3\"]` → одна строка `2 3` → граница теряется. Это не «полагаться/не полагаться», это молчаливая порча входа. На Linux execv передаёт вектор напрямую, там у run_case ответственности ноль — асимметрия платформенная, не твоя.\n\nПлюс важное: без кавычек ломается не только `2 3`, но и `   10`. CreateProcess съест ведущие пробелы, ребёнок получит `10`, и тест на trim станет пустым (зелёный не потому, что парсер тримит, а потому что пробелы не доехали). На Linux он бы реально проверял. Т.е. кавычки определяют, достижим ли вообще тестируемый уровень — «не полагаться на run_case» технически невозможно, он часть смысла теста.\n\nSo the harness must be correct and proven.\n\nЧто делать вместо «не полагаться»: сделать ответственность проверенной.\n- оставить quoting;\n- add a self-test: probe prints argc/argv (or checksum), cases: 1 arg with inner space, 1 arg with leading spaces, 2 args; assert exact vector on both platforms. Then test cases can rely on it.\n- (optional) document contract.\n\nTest hygiene: `_too_much` = list `2 3`; `\"2 3\"` as single arg is a separate case with its own name/expected (it tests \"число с внутренним пробелом\", не «лишние аргументы»).\n\nCaveat about ad-hoc quoting: simple `\"{}\"` is correct only for args without `\"` and not ending with `\\`. For test-controlled inputs that's fine; document as limitation. [ФАКТ про правило, СРЕДНИЙ на полноту]\n\nQuestion: хочешь ли plan из двух частей (harness self-test + гигиена кейсов), или обсудим сначала?\n\nI should be careful about confidence on the Windows parsing details: leading spaces stripped — high confidence. Quotes preserved — high. Backslash/quote rules — high but say check docs (\"Parsing C++ command-line arguments\", Microsoft Learn).\n\nAlso note plan mode: I cannot edit. Good.\n\nLet me write final answer, moderately concise. Avoid overly long. But this is a meaty design question; a few sections fine.\n\nI'll phrase the leading-space insight carefully: does CreateProcess strip leading whitespace in unquoted arg? The command line is a single string; the CRT parser skips whitespace between args. `\"exe\"    10` → argv[1] = \"10\". Yes. With `\"exe\" \"   10\"` → argv[1] = \"   10\". Confirmed by rules. High confidence.\n\nBut careful: run_case currently builds `\"...main.exe\" \"   10\"` (quoted) — so child gets leading spaces. If quotes removed: `\"...main.exe\"    10` → child gets `10`. So the test would still pass (program trims either way), but it would no longer test the trim. Right.\n\nOne more: is it possible CreateProcess itself doesn't parse (the string is passed to child; child's CRT parses)? The child's CRT (`main`) parses via its startup. Either way, the observed effect is the same. I'll say \"парсер командной строки дочернего процесса (CRT)\".\n\nLet me finalize.",
