          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks two things:\n1. Confirm: CRT `_spawnv`/`_execv` will be bad under parallel ctest?\n2. What is `boost::process` and why \"not now\"?\n\nLet me answer.\n\n### 1. `_spawnv`/`_execv` and parallel ctest\n\nFirst, correct my earlier claim about `_spawnv` and redirection. `_spawnv` does NOT take file handles; it inherits the parent's std handles. So to redirect stdin/stdout/stderr to files, you'd have to change the parent's own std handles (`_dup2` on fd 0/1/2), spawn, then restore. That's process-global mutable state.\n\nUnder parallel ctest: multiple `run_case` instances run as separate processes, so each has its own fd table — actually wait, parallel ctest runs separate run_case processes, not threads. Each process's own std handles are its own. So `_dup2` in one run_case process doesn't affect another run_case process.\n\nHmm, so is it really \"bad under parallel ctest\"? Let me reconsider. Parallel ctest = multiple ctest workers = multiple run_case.exe processes. Each run_case has its own CRTL fd table. So mutating its own std fds is process-local, not shared. So the \"race\" argument I made earlier is weaker than I stated.\n\nBut there are still real problems with `_spawnv` + `_dup2`:\n- You lose the `SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES.bInheritHandle` control and `CREATE_ALWAYS`/`OPEN_EXISTING` semantics via CreateFile. Actually `_dup2` just duplicates an fd; you'd still open files with `_open`, which is fine.\n- More importantly: `_spawnv` uses the CRT's std handle inheritance. On Windows, the CRT maps fds to OS handles. `_spawnv` marks the parent's std handles inheritable? Actually `_spawnv` inherits handles that are marked inheritable. The child CRT gets fds 0/1/2 from the parent's. It's designed to work.\n- The real issue: modifying the parent's fd 0/1/2 temporarily is a stateful, error-prone dance (must restore, must handle failure, `_dup2` of inherited handles). And it's per-process so no cross-process race, but within a single run_case it's fine since it's single-threaded.\n- Another real issue: `_spawnv`'s quoting is the CRT's own algorithm, which is actually correct. So the quoting problem is solved. But you get less control.\n\nHmm, so my earlier framing \"race under parallel ctest\" was overclaimed. I should correct myself honestly (the anti-sycophancy applies to my own claims too — I should own the error). Actually wait: is there a genuine issue with `_spawnv` and parallel? Let me think again.\n\nActually there is a subtle Windows issue: handle inheritance. `CreateProcess` with `bInheritHandles=TRUE` makes ALL inheritable handles inherited. The CRT `_spawn` sets up the std handles. But if ctest runs tests in parallel *within the same process*? No, ctest spawns separate processes. So no shared fd table.\n\nSo I should correct: the \"race\" concern I raised is not accurate for process-per-test parallel ctest. The real reasons `_spawnv` is not ideal here are:\n- loss of explicit control over handle inheritance and creation/disposition (you'd use `_open`, which is fine but less explicit);\n- the redirect-via-`_dup2`-on-own-std-fds dance is stateful and messier than `STARTUPINFO`;\n- `_execv` replaces the process image (on Windows `_execv` spawns + exits? Actually on Windows, `_execv` is implemented as spawn + exit of parent, unlike POSIX exec that replaces). Hmm, `_execv` on Windows: the CRT emulates by spawning the child and then exiting the parent. Which is not what run_case wants (run_case needs to wait and check the exit code of the child, then return its own code). `_execv` would terminate run_case itself, losing the ability to compare codes. So `_execv` is wrong for run_case. `_spawnv` returns and is usable with `_P_WAIT`.\n- `_spawnv` with `_P_WAIT` returns the child exit code; but `_spawnv` can't capture... it returns int (child exit code). But it doesn't distinguish signal-style termination well; and error handling is via errno. It also polls (`_P_WAIT`).\n\nBut the biggest practical point: `_spawnv` would remove your direct control over `STARTUPINFO` and `CreateFile` with `FILE_SHARE_READ` / `CREATE_ALWAYS`. You'd reimplement redirection with `_open`/`_dup2`. That's not impossible, just a different (CRT-level) interface.\n\nSo: is it \"плохо при параллельном ctest\"? Honest answer: my race argument was not correct — parallel ctest uses separate processes, each with its own fd table. The real drawbacks are API-control and the stateful `_dup2` dance, plus `_execv` being unsuitable. I should correct myself clearly. This is important for trust.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether there's a genuine parallel concern. If ctest runs tests in parallel, and each run_case modifies *its own* std handles, no conflict. Unless run_case were multi-threaded (it's not). So no race. I'll retract.\n\nBut wait — is there a shared resource issue with `_spawnv` and ctest's own handle inheritance? `_spawn` uses `CreateProcess` internally with `TRUE` for inherit? If ctest's worker process has inheritable handles, and run_case spawns child with inheritance, extra handles could leak to the child. But that's also true with CreateProcess if bInheritHandles=TRUE (which run_case uses). Actually run_case uses `TRUE` at line 209, so it already inherits all inheritable handles. `_spawnv` similar. So no new problem.\n\nSo I'll be honest: the \"parallel\" concern I stated was overstated; the real reasons are control + statefulness + `_execv` semantics.\n\nHmm, but the user said \"так понимаю будет плохо при параллельном ctest\". I should correct the misunderstanding — including that it originated from my own earlier overclaim. That's the right thing per anti-sycophancy.\n\nLet me be careful and precise:\n- `_execv`/`_execve` on Windows: implemented as spawn then `_exit` in the parent — so run_case would exit and couldn't compare/return exit codes. Unsuitable. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ-ish; mark СРЕДНИЙ and suggest checking docs since Windows CRT exec semantics differ]\n- `_spawnv`/`_spawnve` with `_P_WAIT`: usable, CRT does quoting, but redirection requires `_dup2` of run_case's own fd 0/1/2 around the call — stateful, and you lose explicit `CREATE_ALWAYS`/`FILE_SHARE` control. Not a race (separate processes), but messier.\n- So the recommendation stands (CreateProcess + explicit STARTUPINFO), but the *reason* is control/cleanliness, not parallelism.\n\nActually, let me double-check `_execv` on Windows. MSDN: \"The _exec functions ... The _exec functions do not return to the calling process.\" On Windows, the CRT's `_exec` family spawns a new process and exits the current one (it cannot replace the image). Yes. So the parent process terminates. So run_case can't use it. Good.\n\nAnd `_spawn` family: `_P_WAIT` waits and returns the exit status. Uses command line built from argv with proper quoting (the CRT's `_spp`/`argv` quoting). Good.\n\nNow, one more genuine subtlety: `_spawnv`'s child's stdin/stdout/stderr = inherited from run_case's CRT fds. If run_case wants to redirect, `_dup2(fd_file, 0)` etc. But note: `_spawn` might set `bInheritHandles` and CRT handles. It's doable. But there's an even bigger issue: after `_dup2`, run_case's own `stdout` (e.g., its error `printf` to stderr) would go to the child's file — unless restored carefully. Stateful.\n\nSo final.\n\n### 2. What is boost::process and why \"not now\"\n\n`boost::process` is a C++ library (part of Boost) for launching and managing child processes with a portable API: start a process, redirect streams to files/pipes, pass args as a container, set env, wait, get exit code. On Windows it internally handles the command-line quoting correctly; on POSIX it uses `execv`-family. It's an abstraction over exactly the `run_case` launching code you hand-wrote, plus more (async, pipes, termination).\n\nWhy \"not now\":\n- It's a dependency (Boost). You just added vcpkg; adding Boost is heavy (large library), and the course's philosophy is to add tools when a task needs them, not preemptively (§ 5,