          "text": "The user asks a sharp conceptual question: \"if I extract what I have (the quoting) as a function, how does that make it a contract?\"\n\nThis is a good critical question. The honest answer: extracting into a function does NOT by itself create a contract. A function is just a named block of code. A contract is the documented promise + the tests that verify it. Extraction is neither necessary nor sufficient for having a contract.\n\nSo I should be precise and honest:\n\n- Extraction gives you a **name** and a **boundary** — a single place to attach the promise and to point at. That's it.\n- A contract appears when you **write down the promise** (pre/post/invariant) and **verify it with a test**. That can be done on inline code too (comment + probe test).\n- So: вынос ≠ контракт. Вынос = удобная точка, куда контракт можно прикрепить. Контракт = текст обещания + проверка.\n\nActually there's a subtlety: the contract must be *about a unit*. Inline code inside `main` has no callable unit to test directly — you can only test it through the whole `run_case` (via probe). Once extracted as a function, you *could* unit-test `quote_arg` directly (call it with inputs, check the output string) — but that tests the string, not the round-trip through Windows parsing. The real contract (\"child receives exactly s\") can only be verified end-to-end via a probe child anyway. So extraction doesn't even buy direct testability of the semantic contract — only of the syntactic output.\n\nHmm, that's an important and honest point: \n- If contract = \"output string is correct by the documented rules\", then extraction + a table-driven unit test verifies it — but that test encodes the same rules, so it's somewhat circular (you're testing your implementation against your own understanding of the rules).\n- If contract = \"round-trip: child sees exactly s\", the only real test is a probe child — and that works regardless of extraction.\n\nSo extraction's actual benefit: a named boundary + ability to unit-test the output + reuse if second caller appears. The contract itself is documentation + verification, independent of extraction.\n\nI should also address: I earlier said \"вынести + контракт + probe\". The user is poking at whether вынос IS the contract-making act. Answer: no.\n\nLet me also give the crisp definition: a contract has three parts:\n1. text (promise: pre/post/invariant) — documentation;\n2. test that verifies it — executable proof;\n3. a unit to attach it to (function/module) — the addressee. Extraction provides only #3.\n\nSo without #1 and #2, extraction is just refactoring.\n\nI should be direct and critical, and maybe correct my own earlier framing (I listed extraction as part of \"principled improvement\", which could be read as if extraction creates the contract). Own that.\n\nAlso note: in their AGENTS § 4.3, contracts are documented via `@pre`/`@post` comments — so the course's meaning of \"contract\" is primarily documentation of guarantees, verified by tests. Good anchor.\n\nLet me answer concisely but with precision. Include analogy maybe: контракт — это табличка с обещанием; вынос — это столб, на который можно её повесить. Без таблички столб — просто столб.\n\nAnd the crucial nuance: even the probe test doesn't need extraction; and unit-testing the extracted function tests the string, not the Windows parsing round-trip.\n\nLet me also answer what extraction does buy, honestly:\n- single named place → easier to reason/review/document;\n- if a second caller appears → DRY;\n- possible unit test of the output string (partial, syntactic).\n\nAnd what it doesn't buy:\n- doesn't by itself define or enforce any guarantee;\n- doesn't make the semantic round-trip testable (that needs probe);\n- no unit to attach until second caller (course §4.2: wrapper without added value).\n\nKeep it tight. Plan mode, no edits. No tools needed.\n\nLet me write.",
