          "text": "The user shifts to the product side: t4_c and t4_cpp handling of the radius argument. Case: argc=2, so \"everything ok\" count-wise, but argv[1] may be a string. They ask: is it better to do a check before the strtod conversion for \"something bad\" in the radius? Or what's better?\n\nThey want guidance (\"направь меня\"), not the full solution. This is a design question about validation strategy.\n\nLet me think about the current implementations:\n\n**t4_c (main.c:116-143):**\n```c\nchar* end_r;\nerrno = 0;\ndouble r = strtod(argv[1], &end_r);\nconst char* tail = end_r;\nwhile (*tail != '\\0' && isspace((unsigned char)*tail)) ++tail;\nif (end_r == argv[1] || *tail != '\\0' || is_hex_prefix(argv[1])) {\n    // \"не число\"\n}\nif (errno == ERANGE && (r == 0.0 || !isfinite(r))) { // out of range\n}\nif (!isfinite(r)) { // not finite\n}\nif (r <= 0) { // not positive\n}\n```\n\n**t4_cpp (main.cpp:24-63):**\n```cpp\n// trim edges\n// skip leading '+'\ndouble R = 0;\nauto [ptr, ec] = std::from_chars(first, last, R);\nif (ec == std::errc::result_out_of_range) return radius_out_of_range;\nif (ec != std::errc() || ptr != last) return radius_not_number;\nif (!std::isfinite(R)) return radius_not_finite;\nif (R <= 0) return radius_not_positive;\nreturn R;\n```\n\nSo the question: the current approach is \"parse first, then classify based on parse result\". The user asks whether to instead \"check for bad characters before conversion\".\n\nLet me think about the design options:\n\n**Option A (current): parse-first (from_chars/strtod), then classify by ec/ptr/errno.**\n- Pros: no hand-written lexer; the parser already tells you where it stopped and whether it overflowed; fewer places to get the number grammar wrong; consistent with the \"don't reinvent the parser\" principle (§4.2 — don't write wrappers/validators when STL/C library already does it).\n- Cons: the classification is subtle (errno+value for out-of-range on C side; hex prefix special-casing; plus