ining design decision is the error taxonomy/wording (which we discussed earlier), not pre-validation.\n\nHowever, there's one legitimate \"pre\" check that isn't a hand-rolled lexer: the **hex prefix** (`0x`/`0X`) rejection, because `strtod` accepts `0x` while `from_chars` doesn't — they added `is_hex_prefix` on the C side to unify. That's a small targeted special-case, not a general pre-validator. Worth naming as the exception.\n\nAlso the `+` skip on C++ side (from_chars doesn't accept leading `+`, strtod does) — also a targeted compatibility shim.\n\nSo the guidance: \n- Not a general \"check for bad chars before conversion\".\n- Yes targeted shims for documented parser differences (hex prefix on C; leading `+` on C++), and those should live in the shared `common/` parse layer, ideally one function so C and C++ can't drift.\n- Classification order after parse: out_of_range → not_a_number (ec/ptr) → not_finite → not_positive. Keep the order because it matters (e.g., `1e400` must be out_of_range, not not_finite).\n- Also the \"trim edges\" step is fine and needed (whitespace tolerance), and is not a \"bad char\" check.\n\nAlso: the user's concern \"argc=2 and всё ок, но argv[1] может быть строкой\" — this is exactly layer 1 (lexical). So the answer to \"how better\" is: the parse already covers it; don't pre-check.\n\nLet me also address the specific question \"сделать перед strtod-конвертацией проверку, есть ли что-то плохое в радиусе? или как лучше, направь меня\".\n\nGuide with the architectural axes (per §4.2, name the axes before proposing an edit):\n- **Model of errors**: token classification vs pre-validation.\n- **Single source of truth**: parser vs hand scanner.\n- **C/T4 consistency**: same category set for C and C++.\n\nAnd point out the tradeoff: pre-validation creates a second grammar that will drift from the parser's grammar — exactly the kind of C/C++ divergence they've been fighting.\n\nLet me also mention a concrete litmus: for any pre-check you'd want to write, ask \"does the parser already answer this, and more accurately?\" If yes, don't duplicate.\n\nOne more: there's a subtle issue in the current C code order: `errno == ERANGE && (r == 0.0 || !isfinite(r))`. On MSVC, underflow may not set ERANGE, and overflow sets ERANGE with inf. This is a heuristic. Also `strtod` on `1e-400` → 0 with ERANGE on some. They documented this. The C++ side uses `ec == result_out_of_range`. So the taxonomy is unified but the detection differs. Fine.\n\nAnother subtlety: on the C side, `end_r == argv[1]` detects \"no conversion at all\". But if `argv[1]` is empty string, `strtod(\"\")` → end==start → not_number. But earlier we noted the harness can't pass empty arg. Fine.\n\nAlso `is_hex_prefix(argv[1])` — checks raw argv, not after trim. If input is ` 0x10`, hex prefix check on raw string might miss leading spaces... Actually `strtod(\" 0x10\")` skips spaces and parses hex → returns 16, end after. Then `is_hex_prefix(argv[1])` on `\" 0x10\"` would look at first char ` ` — might not detect. Hmm, is that a bug? They have a test `t4_c_rad_space_hex` = `\"   0x10\"` expecting `t4_radius_space_hex` (not number). So `is_hex_prefix` must handle leading whitespace, or the test would fail. Let me not go down this hole unless relevant; but it's a good example of \"the shim needs to be as smart as the parser\". Actually this is a useful point: the targeted shim (`is_hex_prefix`) is itself finicky — another argument for keeping shims minimal and shared, and for preferring `from_chars` (which naturally rejects hex in general mode) over `strtod` where possible. But C side has no from_chars (C17). Hmm.\n\nActually — worth noting: this asymmetry (C needs shims because strtod accepts hex/plus; C++ needs shims because from_chars rejects plus) is precisely why a **shared classification in `common/`** would help, or at least a shared test vector table. This connects to the journal note that `is_hex_prefix` in geometry vs `parse_int32` in string_utils is a layering concern.\n\nBut the user only asked to be guided on the pre-check question. Let me keep focused but mention the axes and a recommendation, then offer to turn it into a plan.\n\nAlso they mentioned earlier the \"число + мусор\" vs \"нет числа\" distinction. The parse-first approach naturally gives you that: `ec==0 && ptr!=last` = number + junk; `ec!=0` = no number. So parse-first actually *enables* the finer taxonomy they wanted. Pre-validation would make it harder. Good argument.\n\nLet me structure the answer:\n1. Direct answer: нет, предварительную проверку «на плохие символы» не надо. Держи parse-first.\n2. Why: parser is the single source of truth for the grammar; a pre-scanner would be a second grammar that drifts (their C/C++ divergence history). And it can't catch range anyway.\n3. The real layers (name the axes): argv shape → lexical → range → finite → domain. Show where each is handled.\n4. What pre-checks ARE legitimate: targeted shims for documented parser differences (leading `+`, hex prefix) — and they should be minimal and shared (common/), not a general validator.\n5. The classifier is exactly what gives you the fine taxonomy you wanted (parse result lets you distinguish no-number vs number+junk vs out-of-range).\n6. Note the order matters (out_of_range before others).\n7. Offer: turn into plan (axis: error taxonomy + where shims live), ask which scope.\n\nMark categories/confidence. Ask clarifying questions.\n\nThe user said \"направь меня\" — guide, don't give fish. So lead with questions/principles, not full code.\n\nLet me also reference their code lines.\n\nKeep it reasonably concise but substantive. Plan mode → no edits, end with questions and offer to plan.\n\nLet me write.\n\nI want to name architectural axes before proposing edits (§4.2 block says: \"Перед тем как предлагать правку — задай вопрос: это локальная правка или нужен архитектурный пересмотр? Если у задачи есть архитектурные оси (модель ошибок, владение ресурсами