ый параметр, соберём всё из argv в одну строку и парсим\". It makes errors consistent with what the user typed after the exe name.\n\nBut then how do you ever report \"лишние аргументы\"? You wouldn't — there's no concept of too many args anymore; everything after exe is \"the radius text\". Is that acceptable? For a single-parameter CLI, it's actually a coherent model: \"one positional string parameter\". Many CLIs work like that (everything after the command is one string). But it contradicts having `argc != 2` checked at all.\n\nSo the design question is real and interesting. Let me lay it out for the user and ask which they want, with the tradeoffs. That's the \"guide me\" they asked for.\n\nTradeoffs:\n- Model 1 (argv-as-is, current): respects shell boundaries; distinguishes \"too many args\" from \"bad content\"; but the two forms of typing `2 abc` differ.\n- Model 2 (join-and-parse): user-visible consistency between `2 abc` and `\"2 abc\"`; but discards the notion of \"too many arguments\" and treats argv as raw text (arguably second-guessing the shell; and `10 20` becomes \"not a number\" rather than \"too many\").\n\nAlso note: Model 2 has a subtlety — joining with a single space: `main.exe 10   20` (two args) → joined \"10 20\"? Actually if you join argv elements with a space, `10` and `20` → \"10 20\" (one space), vs the user typed multiple spaces. You can't reconstruct original spacing. But you don't need to; you just need to classify. Still, it's lossy re-serialization — the very thing we've been careful about.\n\nHmm, and Model 2 reintroduces exactly the argv→string→parse round-trip that we spent the whole conversation de-risking! That's ironic and worth pointing out: to make `2 abc` and `\"2 abc\"` equal, you'd join argv back into a string — recreating the Windows serialization problem on the *product* side. That's a strong argument against Model 2.\n\nSo my guidance: \n- The machine channel (exit code) is already unified (64) — verify that's what \"о