          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks: show what's needed for variant A — can it be put into `exit_codes` or do I recommend a separate file?\n\nI'm in plan mode. I should read the current `common/exit_codes.h/.hpp` to see what they contain, and then propose a plan.\n\nLet me read exit_codes.h and exit_codes.hpp.\n\nThen present the plan for variant A: where to declare the parse codes.\n\nConsiderations:\n- `exit_codes.h/.hpp` holds exit codes (usage=64, data=65, no_input=66, io_fail=74). These are process exit codes, a different concern from parse error categories. Mixing parse codes into exit_codes would conflate two concerns. \n- A separate `common/parse_codes.h/.hpp` (or `parse_result` / `parse_error`) is cleaner.\n\nBut wait — are parse codes and exit codes related? The parse error codes are internal classification; the main maps them to exit codes. They're distinct. So a separate file is cleaner semantically.\n\nHowever, there's a pragmatic consideration: project philosophy §4.2 — don't create a module without added value. A new header for a few enums is small but justified if it's the \"single place\" for numeric-parse codes. Variant A's whole point is to have one place. `exit_codes` is not that place (different concept). So recommend a separate `common/parse_codes.h/.hpp` (or place them in `string_utils.h/.hpp`, which now hosts number parsing... but `PARSE_*` for points is in geometry.h and is point-specific).\n\nHmm. Let me think about what variant A actually entails concretely:\n\nCurrent sets:\n1. `geometry.h`: `PARSE_TOO_FEW..PARSE_OUT_OF_RANGE` (C enum). Used by parse_point and t3/t4 C mains.\n2. `string_utils.h`: `INT_PARSE_NOT_NUMBER, INT_PARSE_OUT_OF_RANGE` (C enum). Used by parse_int32, t1/t2 C mains.\n3. C++ counterparts: `geometry.hpp` has `inline constexpr int parse_*`; `string_utils.hpp` has `inline constexpr int int_parse_*`.\n4. t4 radius codes: local in t4 files (C enum `RADIUS_*`, C++ enum class `radius_error`).\n\nVariant A = \"не сливать смыслы, а свести объявления в одно место\". So move all these code declarations into a single common header per language, e.g. `common/parse_codes.h` (C) and `common/parse_codes.hpp` (C++). But the values/names overlap and could collide (e.g., `PARSE_NOT_NUMBER=2` in geometry vs radius `not_number=1`). If in one file, need distinct names or a nested scheme.\n\nHmm, \"свести объявления в одно место\" — but if names collide (e.g., two `NOT_NUMBER`), we need prefixes. So the unified header would define, e.g.:\n```c\n// C\nenum { PARSE_TOO_FEW=1, PARSE_NOT_NUMBER=2, PARSE_EXTRA=3, PARSE_NOT_FINITE=4, PARSE_OUT_OF_RANGE=5 };  // point\nenum { INT_PARSE_NOT_NUMBER=1, INT_PARSE_OUT_OF_RANGE=2 };  // int\nenum { RADIUS_OK=0, RADIUS_NOT_NUMBER=1, ... };  // radius\n```\nAll in one file. That's just co-location — no semantic merge. Names already distinct (prefixes). Values may differ across sets (that's fine for variant A, since we're not merging semantics).\n\nBut is co-locating actually valuable? It makes drift visible (you see all sets together). Modest value.\n\nAlternatively, Variant A could mean: put them where the corresponding parser lives (which we partly did): int codes with parse_int32 in string_utils; point codes with parse_point in geometry; radius codes with parse_radius... but parse_radius is local to t4. So \"one place\" = per-parser home, not one file. Hmm, that's more like \"each code set lives with its parser\" — which is already the case for int (string_utils) and point (geometry). Radius is the odd one (local to t4 main).\n\nHmm, I described Variant A earlier as \"свести объявления в одно место (например, все коды разбора — в common/)\". So it's a single header.\n\nNow the user asks: can it go into exit_codes or separate file?\n\nMy recommendation: **separate file**, not exit_codes. Reasons:\n- Different concept: exit codes are the process contract (64/65/66/74); parse codes are internal classification categories. Mixing conflates concerns and makes exit_codes grow with every parser.\n- exit_codes.h is meant to be the single place for exit-codes only (§4.9 п.5: \"Exit-коды — один раз в общем заголовке common/exit_codes.h\"). Adding parse categories there violates that purpose.\n- A dedicated `common/parse_codes.h` / `parse_codes.hpp` matches \"a single place for numeric-parse categories\".\n\nBut also consider: maybe the codes are best kept with their parsers (int with parse_int32, point with parse_point, radius with parse_radius) — because each is parser-specific. Then \"one place\" is not literal. Hmm. The user specifically asks about a file. So propose `common/parse_codes.h/.hpp`.\n\nNow the practical plan for Variant A:\n\nFiles to create:\n- `common/parse_codes.h` (C): declares the three C enums (moved from geometry.h, string_utils.h, and t4_filter_c/main.c).\n- `common/parse_codes.hpp` (C++): declares the C++ counterparts (inline constexpr for parse_*, int_parse_*; and the radius enum class).\nWait — but the C++ radius uses `enum class radius_error` which currently is local to t4_filter_cpp/main.cpp. Moving it to a shared header in common/ means t4_cpp includes parse_codes.hpp. Fine.\n\nBut the C radius enum and C++ radius enum class differ in style (enum vs enum class) — both in their respective headers.\n\nThen:\n- `geometry.h`: remove the `PARSE_*` enum, include `parse_codes.h`.\n- `geometry.hpp`: remove `parse_*` inline constexpr, include `parse_codes.hpp`.\n- `string_utils.h`: remove `INT_PARSE_*`, include `parse_codes.h`.\n- `string_utils.hpp`: remove `int_parse_*`, include `parse_codes.hpp`.\n- `t4_filter_c/main.c`: remove local `enum { RADIUS_* }`, include `parse_codes.h`.\n- `t4_filter_cpp/main.cpp`: remove local `enum class radius_error`, include `parse_codes.hpp`.\n\nCMake: `common/CMakeLists.txt` — the static/shared libs already compile geometry + string_utils. `parse_codes.h/.hpp` are header-only (no .c/.cpp), so no CMake change needed (headers are found via include path). But do the libs need to include them? Header-only. Consumers include them. Actually, if geometry.h includes parse_codes.h, then parse_codes.h must be on the include path — it's in common/, same dir, so `#include \"parse_codes.h\"` works relative to the including file. Good. No CMake change.\n\nWait, but `t4_filter_c/main.c` includes headers via relative paths like `\"../common/exit_codes.h\"`. So it would include `\"../common/parse_codes.h\"`. Fine.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: `string_utils.h` is included by t1/t2 mains via relative path. If string_utils.h includes `parse_codes.h` (same directory), `#include \"parse_codes.h\"` resolves relative to string_utils.h's directory (common/). Good.\n\nNow, is a separate header justified vs. just leaving codes with parsers? The value of Variant A: one place to see all numeric-parse categories, reducing drift. Modest. And it moves radius codes out of t4 into common/. \n\nActually, wait: is moving the radius codes to common/ desirable? The C++ `enum class radius_error` is specific to the radius. Putting it in a shared header is fine (t4 only uses it). But does it add value? It co-locates with other parse codes. OK.\n\nAlternative \"Variant A-light\": just move the radius codes from t4 into `string_utils.h/.hpp` (since parse_radius is radius parsing and string_utils is now the parsing home). And leave geometry's PARSE_* with geometry, int's with string_utils. Then \"one place\" isn't literal but codes live with their parsers. Hmm, but the user asked about a file.\n\nLet me present the options honestly:\n- Option A1: dedicated `common/parse_codes.h/.hpp` — all numeric-parse categories in one file (per language). This is the literal \"одно место\".\n- Option A2: keep codes with their parsers (int+radius in string_utils, point in geometry); just move radius codes out of t4 into string_utils. Less literal, fewer files.\n- exit_codes: not appropriate (different concern; §4.9 says exit_codes is for exit codes).\n\nRecommend A1 if they want a single point; note it creates a new header pair (header-only, no CMake change). Or A2 as lighter.\n\nHmm, but the user asked specifically \"можно ли вынести в exit_codes или советуешь отдельный файл?\" — so answer: separate file, not exit_codes, with reasons. Then show what A entails.\n\nLet me also read exit_codes.h/.hpp to be accurate (what's in them, naming style).\n\nLet me read common/exit_codes.h and .hpp.\n\nAlso read geometry.hpp to see the C++ parse codes (for the plan).\n\nLet me read those.\n\nThen present the plan.\n\nLet me do the reads.\n\nAlso check: does `parse_codes` naming conflict with anything? No.\n\nLet me also consider: if parse_codes.h/.hpp are header-only, no CMake change. But wait — the shared/static libs: geometry.c includes parse_codes.h; that's fine. But should parse_codes be part of the lib's public interface? Since geometry.h includes it, it's transitively included. Fine.\n\nOne more: `t4_filter_cpp/main.cpp` includes `\"../common/string_utils.hpp\"` etc. It would include `\"../common/parse_codes.hpp\"`. Fine.\n\nNow, naming: maybe `common/parse_codes.h` / `common/parse_codes.hpp`. Or `parse_result.h`. I'll propose `parse_codes`.\n\nAlso include guards: `.h` uses `#ifndef IRINA_PARSE_CODES_H`; `.hpp` uses `#pragma once` (project convention).\n\nLet me read exit_codes files and geometry.hpp.\n\nLet me do it.",
