          "text": "The user asks: \"а по уровню проекта регресс и probe-self-test будут нормальным шагом? не будет как с boost::process? или типа того\"\n\nThey're asking whether, at the current level of the project, adding a regression test and a probe-self-test is a proportionate/normal step — or would it be like the boost::process situation (i.e., jumping ahead / over-engineering / premature layer).\n\nI'm in plan mode. I should analyze against the project's philosophy (§4.8 \"голая версия с первого дня\", §5 \"не пропускаем слои\", §4.2 don't add wrappers without value). Give an honest assessment: are these tests appropriate now, or premature?\n\nLet me think carefully.\n\n**Probe-self-test for run_case (harness round-trip):**\n- Is this \"голая версия\" or \"промышленная\"? The course §4.8 says: \"голая версия юнит-теста — exe с return 0/1 с первого дня\". A probe exe + a case is exactly the bare version. So it's not premature — it's aligned with layer 6a.\n- But: does the project need it *now*? The harness `run_case` + `quote_arg` is custom code that's already been a source of subtle bugs (the `2\" 3`, trailing `\\`). The contract is documented but untested. Testing your own harness is legitimate and proportionate when the harness has non-trivial logic. And the course says the tests must be trustworthy (the harness itself must be honest). So yes, proportionate.\n- Cost: one tiny exe (~20 lines) + a few cases. No new dependencies. Not like boost::process (which is a big third-party lib + layer jump).\n- Alternative: could extend existing acceptance tests (e.g., the `RAD_INNER_QUOTE` tests) already exercise quote_arg for spaces/quotes partially. Indeed, we already added `t4_radius_inner_quote`, `t4_radius_multi_quote`, `t4_radius_trailing_backslash` which indirectly test quote_arg. So the probe might be partially redundant! That's an important point: the acceptance tests already cover space/quote/backslash passing via the real tasks. A dedicated probe would be more direct/isolated, but the coverage partly exists.\n  - However, those acceptance tests assert on the task's stderr, which is an end-to-end proxy. A probe asserts on argv directly. The difference: if the task's parser changes, the acceptance test still tests quote_arg indirectly. So maybe the probe is not strictly necessary given those tests exist.\n  - So: probe = nice-to-have, not strictly required now, because the `t4_radius_*` cases already exercise the exact quote_arg edge classes. The probe would be a more direct guard, but it's arguably redundant now.\n\n**Regression test `\\v0x10`/`\\f0X10`:**\n- This is a unit-level test for a specific edge we fixed. Per §4.8, unit testing is the bare version (exe return 0/1), and the course explicitly wants unit tests (§6.1 \"Юнит-уровень (6a): чистая логика — ... exe с return 0/1\"). Also the course's \"не оставляй непокрытым\" principle.\n- Cost: tiny. But it requires standing up the first unit-test exe (layer 6a) — which the project hasn't done yet. That's a small infra step but it's exactly on the ladder (layer 6a). So it's proportionate.\n- Is `\\v0x10` worth a permanent test? It's an exotic input (control char in argv). The divergence was real, but extremely rare in practice. So the *specific* test is low-value on its own. But it's part of building the unit-test capability, which *is* valuable. Hmm.\n- Alternative: it could be folded into a broader unit test of `is_hex_prefix` / `parse_radius` classification, not a one-off. That's better.\n\n**Comparison to boost::process:**\n- boost::process = jumping to layer 20 (matrix acceptance) + heavy dependency, before its time. That's premature.\n- probe/regression = staying within layer 6a (bare unit test exe), which is the *next* rung on the ladder and is explicitly part of the course's minimal skills. So NOT like boost::process.\n\n**But is it the right *next* step?** The project just finished: CMake (layer 16), vcpkg/GTest (layers 18-19), and has acceptance tests. Actually, wait — the ladder says layers 16 (CMake), 17 (E2E generator), 18 (vcpkg), 19 (GoogleTest), 20 (matrix acceptance). The project already has GTest smoke (gtest_smoke). So GoogleTest is *available* now (via vcpkg). That changes things: the \"bare unit test exe (6a)\" was for before GoogleTest; now that GTest is available (layer 19), a proper unit test could use GTest. Hmm.\n- So there's a question: should the unit/regression test be a bare exe (6a style) or use GoogleTest (now available)?\n- The course §4.8: голая версия first, промышленная later. But GoogleTest is already installed. Using it now for the first real unit test would be reasonable (it's connected). But then it's a bigger step.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider the project stage: they've done vcpkg + GTest smoke, CMake, Makefile removed. The next layers per §6.1 notes: этап 2 = CMake/vcpkg/GoogleTest + Release + optimization ladder. So actually GoogleTest is part of этап 2, which they're entering. So writing real unit tests with GTest is on the path.\n\nSo the answer: \n- Both a regression test and a probe can be done as unit tests, and now GoogleTest is available → could use it (aligned with §6 layer 19).\n- Whether it's \"premature like boost::process\": No — boost was a layer jump + heavy dependency. Unit tests are on the current/near layer.\n- BUT: is a dedicated probe for run_case necessary? Given the acceptance tests already cover quote_arg's edge cases (inner space/quote/multi-quote/trailing backslash), a dedicated probe is optional/nice-to-have, not compelled. The regression for `\\v0x10` is genuinely uncovered and cheap.\n- Recommendation: \n  - Regression `\\v0x10`/`\\f0X10` as a unit test on `is_hex_prefix`/`parse_radius` — proportionate, do it (goes into a proper unit-test file).\n  - probe for run_case — optional; the harness round-trip is already indirectly covered by acceptance tests; add only if you want a direct harness guard. Not a layer jump either way.\n\nAlso note: §4.8 says bare version from day one; but the project skipped the bare unit test exe and went to GTest smoke. Hmm, the journal shows they added gtest_smoke but no real unit tests. So the first real unit test is pending. Adding a real unit test (with GTest or bare exe) is a normal next step.\n\nActually, let me check: does the project have a unit-test exe pattern yet? They have `tests/run_case`, `tests/check`, `tests/gtest_smoke`. No unit tests for parse functions. §4.8 says \"голая версия: exe с return 0/1\" should be from day one; but at their stage GTest is connected, so they can use GTest.\n\nSo my answer:\n1. Not like boost::process: boost = layer 20 + heavy dep, jumped ahead. Tests = current/near layer (unit testing is minimal-skill requirement; GTest already connected).\n2. Proportionality:\n   - `\\v0x10` regression: yes, cheap and fills a real gap (no unit tests at all yet). Do it.\n   - run_case probe: optional; acceptance tests already cover the quote classes indirectly. A probe is a direct harness guard — nice but arguably redundant now. If added, keep it minimal.\n3. Where to put them: now that vcpkg+GTest are connected, could be GTest tests rather than a bare exe — but note the project's §4.8 bare-first principle; since GTest is here, using it is fine. Or keep the bare-exe approach for a single probe. Ask preference.\n4. Recommendation & question.\n\nAlso mention: don't over-invest — the probe is the kind of thing that can become a \"big test infra\" (like the boost concern) if it grows. Keep it tiny.\n\nLet me also weigh: the course values \"тесты проверяют все три канала\" and harness honesty. The acceptance tests already verify exit codes/stderr/stdout. The harness itself (run_case/check) is trusted. A probe tests the harness; but the harness's correctness is already demonstrated by the acceptance tests (if quote_arg broke, `t4_radius_inner_quote` would fail on Windows). So the marginal value of a probe is low now — the acceptance tests *are* the probe for the quote classes, indirectly.\n\nHmm, but indirect coverage can be weaker (if the task's message changed, the test might still pass for wrong reasons?). No — the test asserts the exact stderr including the received value, so if quote_arg mangled the arg, the message would differ and the test would fail. So it's a solid indirect guard. So probe = redundant-ish.\n\nConclusion: \n- `\\v0x10` regression: worth it (real gap, cheap).\n- run_case probe: not needed now; the `t4_radius_inner_quote` / `multi_quote` / `trailing_backslash` cases already cover the round-trip edge classes. Adding a probe would be extra infra without new coverage → closer to the \"over-engineering\" risk you're worried about.\n\nThat's a nuanced, honest answer that respects the user's concern.\n\nLet me write concisely with category marks and a recommendation, and ask.\n\nAlso mention the \"голая версия\" vs GTest: they could add the regression as a GTest (since connected) or a bare exe; recommend GTest now that it's available, or keep minimal.\n\nLet me write in Russian, clear, no stray chars.\n\nPlan mode: no edits, no tools. Just text.\n\nLet me draft.",
