          "text": "The user asks: \"ок тогда регресс будет сделан через gtest. а probe-self-test зачем вообще делать если вот те и так покрывают?\"\n\nSo: regression via gtest (decision), and they challenge: why do probe-self-test at all if those (t4_radius_*) already cover it?\n\nThis is consistent with what I said — I said probe is \"not needed now\". So I should agree, concisely, and give the one honest caveat: what a probe would add that the indirect tests don't, so they can decide if that residual value is worth it. And conclude: don't do it now, keep it conditional.\n\nLet me articulate the residual (small) value of a probe vs the indirect coverage:\n- The t4_radius_* tests verify quote_arg indirectly: they assert the exact task stderr. If quote_arg mangles the arg, stderr differs → fail. So they're a valid guard.\n- What they DON'T isolate: if the task's parsing/message changes, the test could mask a harness regression? No — it asserts the received value, so mangling still fails. But:\n  - They only cover the classes that appear in t4 radius arguments (space, one quote, multiple quotes, trailing backslash). They don't cover other argv shapes (e.g., empty arg — not expressible anyway; tabs; leading spaces; an arg that is itself quoted-like).\n  - They're coupled to the task: if t4's semantics change, the expected files change; but that's unrelated to harness correctness.\n  - A probe would test the harness in isolation, decoupling harness correctness from task correctness, and could cover a table of argv shapes exhaustively.\n- But: is that residual worth building an exe + cases now? Given no direct need, no. Keep it deferred/conditional.\n\nSo my answer: You're right — not worth doing now. The only thing a probe adds is *isolation* and exhaustive argv-shape coverage independent of a task; it's not needed while t4_radius_* already exercise the same quote_arg edge classes. So: don't do it; keep as conditional trigger (second consumer of quote_arg, or new arg shapes).\n\nSince plan mode: no edits. Just answer concisely. Maybe note the regression via gtest is the remaining do-item, and offer to plan it (but not execute).\n\nAlso, the user decided regression via gtest. I should perhaps outline what the gtest regression would look like (brief plan) since that's the plan mode purpose. But they only asked the probe question. I can briefly confirm and optionally outline. Keep concise.\n\nLet me answer:\n1. Agree: probe not needed; t4_radius_* cover it.\n2. The only thing a probe adds (isolation + arbitrary argv shapes), and why that's not worth it now.\n3. When it'd become worth it (trigger).\n4. Regression via gtest: confirm; maybe brief note on what it'll test (is_hex_prefix(\"\\v0x10\")==1 / parse_radius → not_number). Offer to prepare a plan.\n\nKeep it tight. Plan mode, no tools needed.\n\nLet me write.",
